cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Recovered target-cell annotation for the DBAASP hemolytic activity records
  used by the AMPBench-MT and QMAP HC50 benchmarks
abstract: >-
  Both the AMPBench-MT and QMAP HC50 regression targets aggregate DBAASP
  hemolytic/cytotoxic activity records while discarding the identity of the cell
  the assay was performed on. AMPBench-MT retains a two-valued cell_category and
  a target-cell count but not the names; QMAP retains only [min, max, consensus]
  with no cell field, while keeping full species names for its bacterial MIC
  targets. This annotation recovers the discarded field from DBAASP for every
  record either benchmark consumes, keyed by DBAASP's own identifiers, together
  with the donor taxon, whether the erythrocytes are nucleated, the reported
  concentration unit and the verbatim lysis endpoint. It also recovers a second
  discarded field: the CENSORING. DBAASP records a concentration as free text,
  where ">256" means the assay never reached 50% lysis at the highest
  concentration tested, and both benchmarks collapse such a bound to a point.
  1,503 of 5,461 records (27.5%) are right-censored, and 1,171 of the 3,142
  erythrocyte records at a 50% lysis endpoint are. Recording a bound as a point
  overstates toxicity, selectively, for the least hemolytic peptides. The
  annotation is intended to make per-target-cell and censoring-aware evaluation
  possible; conditioning a model on the target cell is not proposed as a route
  to higher predictive accuracy, while honouring the censoring measurably is.
type: dataset
authors:
